{# The HTML alternative of `password_changed.txt.twig` (read that file's comment first). Standalone
   for the same reasons as `reset_password.html.twig`: no `base.html.twig`, inline styles only, no
   images, scripts, fonts or remote CSS — nothing that fetches, so nothing that can tell a third
   party this address is live. #}
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
    <head>
        <meta charset="UTF-8">
        <title>Your muzbar password was changed</title>
    </head>
    <body style="font-family: Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.5; color: #222;">
        <h1 style="font-size: 20px;">Your muzbar password was changed</h1>

        {# Timezone pinned on the filter — see `reset_password.txt.twig` for why the default would
           eventually make the word "UTC" untrue. #}
        <p>The password for the muzbar account with this address was changed on
            {{ changedAt|date('Y-m-d H:i', 'UTC') }} UTC:
            <strong>{{ recipientEmail }}</strong>.</p>

        <p>If this was you, you don't need to do anything.</p>

        {# Full URL as the visible link text, as in the reset mail: a recipient who suspects
           something is wrong is exactly the one who should be able to see where a link goes. #}
        <p>If you did not make this change, reset your password straight away:</p>

        <p><a href="{{ forgotPasswordUrl }}">{{ forgotPasswordUrl }}</a></p>

        <p>Then check the security of this email account too &mdash; anyone who can read it can
            request a password reset.</p>

        <hr>

        <p style="color: #666; font-size: 13px;">This is an automated message from muzbar. Replies to
            it are not read.</p>
    </body>
</html>
